---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import GuideData from "../../components/GuideData.astro";
import EveryCut from "../../components/EveryCut.astro";
import { bySlug, kicker } from "../../dossiers/registry.js";
import { dateStamp } from "../../dossiers/display.js";
const D = bySlug["jobs"];
import { period, word } from "../../dossiers/periods.js";
import cuts from "../../../data/series/plfs-cuts.json";
import qtr from "../../../data/series/plfs-quarterly.json";
// periods and counts named in the copy, read from the bundles (src/dossiers/periods.js)
const PM = period("plfs-monthly"), PC = period("plfs-cuts");
const cap = (t) => t[0].toUpperCase() + t.slice(1);
const C = cuts.series, lastC = (k) => C[k].data.at(-1);
const urGap = C.ur_female_urban.data.map((v, i) => v - C.ur_male_urban.data[i]);
const minGap = Math.min(...urGap);
const lfFU = C.lfpr_female_urban.data;
// the quarterly bulletin's latest quarter, and its range over the last three years
const QN = ["Jan–Mar", "Apr–Jun", "Jul–Sep", "Oct–Dec"];
const qLabel = (iso) => `${QN[Math.floor((+iso.slice(5, 7) - 1) / 3)]} ${iso.slice(0, 4)}`;
const qUr = qtr.series.ur.data, qDates = qtr.dates;
const q12 = qUr.slice(-12).filter((v) => v != null);
const q12From = qDates[qDates.length - 12].slice(0, 4);
---
<Base
  title="Jobs · India Atlas"
  description={D.description}
  nav="guides"
  dateStamp={dateStamp(D)}
  crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / ${D.section} / <b>${D.crumbTitle}</b>`}
  crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`}
  canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/jobs/"
  ogImage={`/og/${D.slug}.png`}
>
<div class="page">

  <div class="titleblock break">
    <div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div>
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <h1 set:html={D.headline} />
      <p class="lede">India's jobs number is not a count of jobs. It is a survey of people, what they did last week, what they did last year, and the answer changes depending on how you ask. This guide keeps the three answers side by side.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Source <b>MoSPI, NSO</b></span>
      <span>Frequency <b>Monthly, quarterly, annual</b></span>
      <span>Population <b>Age 15 and above</b></span>
      <span>Coverage <b>All-India &amp; States/UTs</b></span>
      <span>Latest print <b>{PM.long}</b></span>
      <span>Bulletin <b>Monthly, from Apr 2025</b></span>
    </div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Unemployment, monthly, current weekly status (CWS)</div><div class="v acc" id="n-ur">–</div><div class="d" id="d-ur">–</div><div class="nspark" id="nspark-ur"></div></div>
       <div class="num"><div class="l">Participation, LFPR</div><div class="v" id="n-lfpr">–</div><div class="d" id="d-lfpr">–</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Urban women, unemployment rate (UR)</div><div class="v" id="n-fur">–</div><div class="d" id="d-fur">–</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Annual UR, usual status</div><div class="v" id="n-psss">–</div><div class="d" id="d-psss">–</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- § 01 current reading -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The current reading</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Monthly bulletin, {PM.long}</span></div>
    <div class="reading-top">
      <div><span class="rk">Current reading</span><div class="big" id="rb-big">–</div></div>
      <p class="rt-cap">Unemployment rate, {PM.long}: everyone aged 15+ who had no work last week, was looking, and could start. The monthly bulletin is {PM.nWord} months old; treat any single month accordingly.</p>
      <div class="rt-side"><span class="rk">A year into the bulletin</span><div class="rt-side-v" id="rb-first">–</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="reading-cols">
      <div class="reading-col">
        <span class="mk">What the print says</span>
        <Prose slot="jobs.current-reading.what-the-print-says" />
      </div>
      <div class="reading-col now">
        <span class="mk">What it does not say</span>
        <Prose slot="jobs.current-reading.what-it-does-not-say" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 register -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>The register of series</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Latest, previous</span></div>
    <div class="break ledger">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Series</th><th>Latest</th><th>vs prev.</th><th>Year ago</th><th class="spark">History</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="ledger-body"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 three stopwatches -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>One question, three stopwatches</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">per cent, annual, agriculture years</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-annual" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Last week or last year: the answer depends on the question</h3>
            <div class="sub">All-India unemployment rate, 15+, current weekly status vs usual status (PS+SS), 2017-18 to 2023-24</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-annual">–</div><div class="c">2023-24, usual status</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-annual">
            <button class="chip" data-key="ur_cws"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Weekly status, CWS</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="ur_psss"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Usual status, PS+SS</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-annual"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, PLFS annual reports, agriculture years (Jul–Jun) plotted at July, the gap between the lines is underemployment: work that exists for the year but not for the week</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Ask about the whole year, did you work, more often than not, and 3.2 per cent of the labour force says no. Ask about last week and the answer is roughly double. The vertical distance between these lines is the casualisation of Indian work: hundreds of millions who are employed in principle and idle in practice, week to week.</p>
            <p>Both lines fall through the window. The post-pandemic recovery in work is real, on both stopwatches: the question each answers is simply different.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The annual series ends 2023-24</span>
            <p>Annual usual-status unemployment has halved in six years, from <b id="ex-p0">–</b> to <b id="ex-p1">–</b>. The weekly measure fell in step and remains near twice the annual one: the recovery closed neither the gap nor the question of job quality.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-lfpr-ann" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Seven years of joining the count</h3>
            <div class="sub">All-India labour force participation rate, 15+, usual status (PS+SS) vs weekly status (CWS), 2017-18 to 2023-24</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-lfpr-ann">–</div><div class="c">2023-24, usual status</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-lfpr-ann">
            <button class="chip" data-key="lfpr_psss"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Usual status, PS+SS</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="lfpr_cws"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Weekly status, CWS</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-lfpr-ann"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, PLFS annual reports, agriculture years (Jul–Jun) plotted at July, the monthly participation plate (§06) runs from Apr 2025; this annual view runs seven years and shows the structural rise the bulletin cannot yet see</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The monthly participation plate in §06 is {PM.nWord} months old and shows a snapshot. This annual companion runs seven agriculture years and shows the trend: participation has climbed steeply and steadily, on both stopwatches. Most of the climb is rural women re-entering the count, through self-employment, as the next section shows, not urban job-seekers flooding in.</p>
            <p>The usual-status line sits above the weekly one because it catches attachment to work across the year, not just the week surveyed. Both rise in lockstep: the recovery in participation is real on either stopwatch.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The annual series ends 2023-24</span>
            <p>Usual-status LFPR rose from <b id="ex-lf0">–</b> to <b id="ex-lf1">–</b>: <b id="ex-lf-d">–</b> in seven years. The weekly measure rose from <b id="ex-lfc0">–</b> to <b id="ex-lfc1">–</b>. No fifteen-month bulletin could show a shape this clear.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 plate 1 -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>Plate 1: the long arc, urban</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">per cent, quarterly, CWS</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-main" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Eight years of urban unemployment, including the quarter everything stopped</h3>
            <div class="sub">Urban unemployment rate, 15+, current weekly status, 2018 to 2026</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-main">–</div><div class="c">Jan–Mar 2026, urban CWS</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-main"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, PLFS quarterly bulletins, gaps: Apr–Jun 2019 (a mislabeled source cell, the published figure is 8.9; quarantined, disclosed in metadata) and Jan–Mar 2025 (never published, design transition)</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The quarterly bulletin is the oldest continuous stopwatch: urban India, last-week status, since 2018. The spike is the lockdown quarter: one urban worker in five counted unemployed in April–June 2020. What matters after the spike is the slow grind down: six years to retrace a few points.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the {qLabel(qDates.at(-1))} quarter</span>
            <p>The urban rate sits at <b id="ex-q1">–</b>, against <b id="ex-q0">–</b> when the bulletin began. Most of that fall is behind it: since {q12From} the rate has held between {Math.min(...q12).toFixed(1)}% and {Math.max(...q12).toFixed(1)}%.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 05 the measure -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">05</span><h2>What this measures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Three ratios, one survey. The labour force is everyone working or actively seeking work. The participation rate, LFPR, is that group as a share of the population. The worker-population ratio is the share actually working. Unemployment is the gap between them: seekers as a share of seekers-plus-workers. When we say unemployment is 5.5 per cent, we mean <b>5.5 per cent of the labour force</b>: not of the country.</p>
        <p>The status questions do the real work. Usual status asks about the reference year and catches attachment to work; current weekly status asks about the seven days before the surveyor's knock and catches idleness inside employment. India publishes both because both are true.</p>
        <p>And it is a survey: sample-based, household by household. The monthly bulletin is young, the quarterly one is urban-only in its long form, and the annual one runs on agriculture years. They are three instruments, and this guide never splices them into one line.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>Unemployment needs a seeker.</b> A person who stops looking leaves the labour force: and the rate falls without a single job created. Read UR beside LFPR, always. </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>Low is not the same as good.</b> In an informal economy, open unemployment is a luxury good. The Indian rate understates distress by construction.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iii</span>
          <b>One bad cell, quarantined.</b> The source carries a mislabeled 2019 cell contradicting its own WPR/LFPR identity; we exclude it from the chart and keep it in the raw archive.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 06 component plates -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">06</span><h2>The parts, one plate each</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">per cent, monthly bulletin</span></div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-cuts" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Four labour markets in one country</h3>
            <div class="sub">Unemployment rate by gender × sector, CWS, {PC.span}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-cuts">–</div><div class="c">{PC.short}, urban women</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-cuts"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, PLFS monthly bulletin, 15+, current weekly status</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The national rate is an average of four very different markets. Urban women seek work in a market twice as tight as urban men's; the rural lines bunch lower, where work is seasonal, family-based and rarely "sought" in the survey's sense. The monthly bulletin is young, so read the levels more than the wiggles.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {PC.stamp}</span>
            <p>Urban women: <b id="ex-fu">–</b>. Urban men: <b id="ex-mu">–</b>. {minGap >= 2 ? `The gap has stayed above two points in every one of the bulletin's ${PC.nWord} months: a structure, not a fluctuation.` : `The gap has ranged from ${minGap.toFixed(1)} to ${Math.max(...urGap).toFixed(1)} points over the bulletin's ${PC.nWord} months.`}</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-lfpr" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The participation chasm</h3>
            <div class="sub">Labour force participation rate by gender × sector, CWS, {PC.span}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-lfpr">–</div><div class="c">{PC.short}, urban women</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-lfpr"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, PLFS monthly bulletin, share of the population working or seeking work</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>This is the more important chart. Three-quarters of urban men are in the labour force; one urban woman in four is. Rural women participate more, farm and family enterprise absorb labour the city does not, but still at barely half the male rate. No single policy moves this line; childcare, safety, transport and social permission all move it at once.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {PC.stamp}</span>
            <p>Urban women's participation: <b id="ex-fl">–</b>. Over the bulletin's {PC.nWord} months it has moved only between {Math.min(...lfFU).toFixed(1)}% and {Math.max(...lfFU).toFixed(1)}%. Its gap to urban men's participation, {(lastC("lfpr_male_urban") - lastC("lfpr_female_urban")).toFixed(1)} points, is the widest in this cut.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-wpr" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Who is actually working</h3>
            <div class="sub">Worker population ratio by gender × sector, CWS, {PC.span}, the gap between this line and the participation line above is the unemployment rate</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-wpr">–</div><div class="c">{PC.short}, urban men</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-wpr"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, PLFS monthly bulletin, the share of the population actually at work, distinct from the share willing to work (LFPR)</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Participation asks who is in the pool; this asks who is actually in a job. The two lines move together for men, because most men who want work find it. They part sharply for women: many women counted as participating are still searching, so the WPR line sits well below the LFPR line. That vertical gap, read alongside the UR plate, is the unemployment rate made visible.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {PC.stamp}</span>
            <p>{lastC("wpr_male_urban").toFixed(1)}% of urban men are at work, against {lastC("wpr_female_urban").toFixed(1)}% of urban women. The male lines hold steady; the female lines are where a recovering labour market would show it: a WPR rising without LFPR rising means existing seekers are finding work, not new ones joining.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 07 what work is -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">07</span><h2>What work is</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">share of workers, per cent, annual reports</span></div>

    <div class="break basket">
      <div class="bar" id="bk-st-bar"></div>
      <div class="labels" id="bk-st-labels"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="break basket" style="margin-top:26px">
      <div class="bar" id="bk-in-bar"></div>
      <div class="labels" id="bk-in-labels"></div>
      <p class="note">Two ways to split the same workforce, 2023-24. By status: a majority works for itself. By industry: the farm still employs more Indians than any other place: on the weekly stopwatch, where seasonal work is counted as it happens.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:72px">
      <figure id="cb-status" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The self-employed majority, the salaried minority</h3>
            <div class="sub">Share of workers by broad status in employment, 15+, usual status (PS+SS), 2017-18 to 2023-24</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-status">–</div><div class="c">2023-24, self-employed share</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-status">
            <button class="chip" data-key="self_employed_person"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Self-employed</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="regular_person"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Regular wage/salary</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="casual_person"><i style="background:#A3A9B4"></i>Casual labour</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-status"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, PLFS annual reports, shares of all workers, person, rural + urban, the three statuses sum to 100, agriculture years plotted at July</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The unemployment rate counts people without work. This plate counts the kinds of work the employed actually have. Self-employment, the farm, the shop, the loom, is the Indian norm; the salaried payroll job is the minority; casual labour is the day-rate rest. The underemployment gap in §03 lives here: own-account work absorbs people a payroll never would, at whatever the week offers.</p>
            <p>Rising self-employment is not simply rising enterprise. Part of it is unpaid: helpers in household enterprises are counted as self-employed workers, and earn no separate wage at all.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The 2023-24 register</span>
            <p>Self-employment is <b id="ex-st-se">–</b> of all workers: <b id="ex-st-oa">–</b> own-account and employers, <b id="ex-st-hp">–</b> household helpers. Regular wage work is <b id="ex-st-rg">–</b>; casual labour <b id="ex-st-cs">–</b>. Since 2017-18 the self-employed share has risen <b id="ex-st-dse">–</b> while casual work fell <b id="ex-st-dcs">–</b>: the recovery in work has been a movement into working for oneself, not onto payrolls.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-gender" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The same workforce, asked by gender</h3>
            <div class="sub">Share of workers by status in employment, usual status (PS+SS), men (left) vs women (right), 2017-18 to 2023-24, same colours, same scale</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-gender">–</div><div class="c">2023-24, women self-employed</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend">
            <button class="chip" style="cursor:default"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Self-employed</button>
            <button class="chip" style="cursor:default"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Regular wage</button>
            <button class="chip" style="cursor:default"><i style="background:#A3A9B4"></i>Casual labour</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:32px">
            <div id="plot-gender-male"></div>
            <div id="plot-gender-female"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, PLFS annual reports, shares of all male and all female workers separately, each panel sums to 100, same three colours as the person-level plate above, agriculture years plotted at July</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Split the person-level plate above by gender and the averages dissolve. Men hold steady across the three statuses: roughly half self-employed, a quarter salaried, a fifth casual, barely moving in seven years. Women do not. Female self-employment has climbed fifteen points, almost entirely into unpaid household-enterprise helper roles; female regular-wage work has fallen by five. Women are not joining the salaried minority: they are joining the family firm.</p>
            <p>Read the two right-hand lines together: female casual labour has fallen faster than male. The woman leaving casual work is not moving to a salary; she is moving to a family enterprise that pays no separate wage at all.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The 2023-24 register, by gender</span>
            <p>Self-employment: <b id="ex-g-se-m">–</b> of men, <b id="ex-g-se-f">–</b> of women: up from <b id="ex-g-se-f0">–</b> in 2017-18. Regular wage: <b id="ex-g-rg-m">–</b> of men against <b id="ex-g-rg-f">–</b> of women. Casual labour: <b id="ex-g-cs-m">–</b> of men, <b id="ex-g-cs-f">–</b> of women. The female self-employment line is the structural story behind the participation rise in §03.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 08 what work pays -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">08</span><h2>What work pays</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">₹, nominal, annual reports, quarterly grain</span></div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-pay" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The pay hierarchy: a month of work, four prices</h3>
            <div class="sub">Regular wage/salary earnings (₹ per month) and gross earnings from self-employment (₹ per 30 days), by gender, rural + urban, 2017-18 to 2023-24</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-pay">–</div><div class="c">Apr–Jun 2024, regular wage, person</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-pay">
            <button class="chip" data-key="regular_male"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Regular, men</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="regular_female"><i style="background:#878D99"></i>Regular, women</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="self_employed_male"><i style="background:#A3A9B4"></i>Self-employed, men</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="self_employed_female"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Self-employed, women</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-pay"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, PLFS annual reports, nominal ₹, all-India, regular wages are per month, self-employment earnings per 30 days, near-equal windows, plotted together; the casual day-rate has its own plate below, 2017-18 all-India aggregates quarantined (see provenance)</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The unemployment rate says who lacks work. This plate says what the work that exists is worth. Four lines, one shape: the salaried man on top; the salaried woman and the self-employed man close enough to trade places; the self-employed woman far beneath all three. The gaps are the structure: they have not closed anywhere in the window.</p>
            <p>These are nominal rupees. Some of the climb is pay; some is the price level climbing with it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of Apr–Jun 2024</span>
            <p>A regular job pays <b id="ex-pay-rg">–</b> a month for men, <b id="ex-pay-rgf">–</b> for women. Self-employment grosses <b id="ex-pay-se">–</b> per 30 days for men and <b id="ex-pay-sef">–</b> for women: the widest gap on the plate, roughly <b id="ex-pay-ratio">–</b> between the salaried man and the self-employed woman. Most of the self-employed majority in §07 lives on the lower two lines.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-casual" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The casual day</h3>
            <div class="sub">Average wage per day from casual labour, other than public works, rural + urban, 2017-18 to 2023-24</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-casual">–</div><div class="c">Apr–Jun 2024, person, per day</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-casual">
            <button class="chip" data-key="casual_person"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Person</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="casual_male"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Men</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="casual_female"><i style="background:#A3A9B4"></i>Women</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-casual"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, PLFS annual reports, nominal ₹ per day, all-India, casual work is priced by the day, so it is never drawn on the monthly axis above</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>This is the price of a day's labour for the fifth of Indian workers hired by the day. It has no leave, no contract and no next week attached. The male and female lines never touch: the day is priced differently depending on whose it is.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of Apr–Jun 2024</span>
            <p>A casual day pays <b id="ex-cas-p">–</b>: <b id="ex-cas-m">–</b> for men, <b id="ex-cas-f">–</b> for women. Seven years earlier it paid <b id="ex-cas-0">–</b>; the rise is <b id="ex-cas-r">–</b> in nominal terms, over years in which the cost of living rose too. At twenty-six days a month, the casual month comes to <b id="ex-cas-mo">–</b>: about half the salaried one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 09 the educated queue -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">09</span><h2>The educated queue</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">per cent, annual, usual status</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-edu" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Unemployment climbs the ladder of degrees</h3>
            <div class="sub">Unemployment rate by general education level, 15+, usual status (PS+SS), 2023-24, with 2017-18 as pale dots</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-edu">–</div><div class="c">2023-24, graduates</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend">
            <span class="chip" style="cursor:default"><i style="background:#5F6675;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:50%"></i>2023-24</span>
            <span class="chip" style="cursor:default"><i style="background:#C9CED6;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:50%"></i>2017-18</span>
            <span class="chip" style="cursor:default"><i style="background:#2E4BD8;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:50%"></i>Twice the all-India rate or more</span>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-edu"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, PLFS annual reports, all-India, person, rural + urban, 15+, rows in ladder order, not sorted by value, the vertical rule is the all-India rate, every level of education together</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>In most rich countries, unemployment falls as schooling rises. In India it rises with every rung. The reason is the denominator's cousin from §05: open unemployment needs a seeker who can wait. A worker who never went to school cannot afford a jobless week and takes whatever the day offers; a graduate, often young, often with a family behind them, queues for the formal post the degree was meant to buy. The ladder measures who can afford to search, and who cannot afford not to work.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The 2023-24 register</span>
            <p>Graduate unemployment prints at <b id="ex-edu-g">–</b> against <b id="ex-edu-nl">–</b> for those who never learned to read: <b id="ex-edu-x">–</b> the rate, in the same country, the same year. The queue is young as well as educated: among 15-to-29-year-olds the rate is <b id="ex-edu-y">–</b>, against <b id="ex-edu-all">–</b> for everyone 15 and above. The concession the window offers: the graduate rate has fallen from <b id="ex-edu-g0">–</b> in 2017-18, and the youth rate from <b id="ex-edu-y0">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 10 states -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">10</span><h2>A country of labour markets</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">per cent, annual, States/UTs</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-states" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Thirty-six markets, one national average</h3>
            <div class="sub">Unemployment rate by State/UT, 15+, usual status (PS+SS) beside weekly status (CWS), 2023-24</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-states">–</div><div class="c">2023-24, spread, highest to lowest, PS+SS</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend">
            <span class="chip" style="cursor:default"><i style="background:#5F6675;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:50%"></i>Usual status, PS+SS</span>
            <span class="chip" style="cursor:default"><i style="background:#C9CED6;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:50%"></i>Weekly status, CWS</span>
            <span class="chip" style="cursor:default"><i style="background:#2E4BD8;width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:50%"></i>Twice the All-India rate or more</span>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-states"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, PLFS annual report 2023-24, States/UTs, 15+, rural + urban, usual status sorts the strip; the pale dot is the same year on the weekly stopwatch, paired, never averaged, Dadra &amp; Nagar Haveli and Daman &amp; Diu merged in 2020 and appear as one UT</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Each row is a State or Union Territory, sorted by the annual usual-status rate: the dark dot. The pale dot is the same year asked about last week instead; the two stopwatches sit side by side and are never averaged. The vertical rule is All-India. Blue marks the places printing at twice the national rate or more.</p>
            <p>The top of the strip is not simply distress, and the bottom is not simply health. Open unemployment needs a seeker who can afford to wait: Kerala's graduates queue for formal work and are counted; Gujarat's farms and workshops absorb almost everyone at some wage, and the queue never forms. A high rate can mean a labour market worth waiting for. A low one can mean an economy too poor to permit the wait. The strip ranks the searching, not the suffering.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The 2023-24 register</span>
            <p>The spread is the story. <b id="st-max">–</b> prints the highest usual-status rate and <b id="st-min">–</b> the lowest: <b id="st-spread">–</b> apart, around an All-India figure of <b id="st-ai">–</b>. Kerala stands at <b id="st-kerala">–</b> against Gujarat's <b id="st-gujarat">–</b>: one educated labour force searching, one informal economy absorbing. Of the thirty-six, <b id="st-nout">–</b> cross twice the national rate.</p>
            <p>Against 2022-23, the largest fall is <b id="st-fall">–</b>; the largest rise is <b id="st-rise">–</b>. A one-year state move in a sample survey deserves suspicion before applause: the small UTs swing hardest for exactly that reason.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 11 misread -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">11</span><h2>How to misread this number</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Unemployment is 5.5%, so 94.5% have jobs."</h3>
          <p>The denominator is the labour force, not the population. With participation at 54 per cent, the employed share of everyone aged 15+ is the worker-population ratio: about 51 per cent. Nearly half the working-age population is outside the count entirely, most of them women.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The rate fell, so things improved."</h3>
          <p>Maybe. Or seekers gave up and left the labour force, which also lowers the rate. The pair to watch is unemployment <em>and</em> participation; one without the other is a Rorschach test.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The monthly, quarterly and annual numbers disagree."</h3>
          <p>They measure differently: last week versus the usual year, urban-only versus all-India. A weekly urban rate beside an annual all-India rate is not a contradiction; it is two instruments reading two things. Never chart them as one line.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iv.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"3% unemployment is full employment."</h3>
          <p>Open unemployment in India is rationed by poverty: most people cannot afford to seek without working. The usual-status rate coexists with vast underemployment, which is precisely the gap between the two stopwatches in §03.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">v.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The survey counts the informal sector."</h3>
          <p>It counts <em>people</em>, including the informally employed: but employment here is a yes, not a description. A street vendor working two hours a day is employed in these tables. The annual reports do price the work (§08 draws on them); hours, contracts and security are thinner ground. A job and a good job are different facts.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

